  metaDesc: 'How many watts does a refrigerator use? A typical fridge draws 100–250 running watts and 800–1,200 starting watts. Calculate annual kWh and running cost by model.',
  keywords: ['how many watts does a refrigerator use', 'refrigerator wattage', 'fridge power consumption', 'refrigerator watts', 'how many watts does a fridge use', 'refrigerator kwh per year'],
  formulaPill: 'Annual kWh = W × duty hours/day × 365 ÷ 1000',
  lead: 'A typical home <strong>refrigerator uses about 100–250 running watts</strong>, but its compressor only runs part of the time — roughly 8 duty-cycle hours a day. That works out to around 400–600 kWh a year for a modern model. Enter your fridge\'s running watts below to see annual energy and cost.',
  reviewed: 'September 5, 2026',
  featureList: ['Refrigerator running watts and starting watts', 'Annual and monthly kWh estimates', 'Electricity cost by model type', 'Compressor duty-cycle aware'],
  methodology: 'Because a refrigerator compressor cycles on and off, we model consumption using an equivalent duty-cycle: the number of hours per day the compressor effectively runs at its rated wattage. Annual energy = running watts × duty hours/day × 365 ÷ 1000. Cost multiplies annual kWh by your electricity rate. Defaults (150 W, 8 duty hours) reflect a modern ENERGY STAR top-freezer unit.',
